      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a25.40.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g25.35.11', 'g25.40.19', 'g25.40.20'],
          lemma: "ξένος ἤμην … τῶν ἐλαχίστων, ἐμοὶ ἐποιήσατε",
          title: "“You did it to me” — where the King says he lives",
          body: "The last teaching Jesus gives before the passion begins is a scene of final judgment, and its criterion should be allowed to land with full surprise: not creed recited, ecstasy experienced, or miracles performed (7:22 already disqualified that list), but **six concrete mercies** — food, drink, welcome, clothing, a sick-visit, a prison-visit. Nothing on the list requires money beyond bread, or talent beyond showing up; the six works are democratically available to every human who has ever lived. The theological bombshell is the ground given for it: **ἐμοὶ ἐποιήσατε** — *you did it to me*. The King does not say the hungry are his concern, his assignment, even his beloved; he says the hungry are *where he is*. It is the Emmanuel theme (1:23) driven to its terminus: God-with-us turns out to mean God-with-the-least, and every act of mercy has, unknown to the actor, a second recipient. Who are ”the least of these my brothers”? Scholars genuinely divide: the narrower reading (Matthew's usage of ἀδελφοί, 12:49-50; 10:40-42) hears *Christ's envoys* — the judgment of nations by how they received the shabby missionaries of a crucified king; the broader reading, which the church's saints have overwhelmingly lived by, hears *every needy person*. The two are not enemies — the narrow is the test case of the broad — and on either reading the address of Christ in the world remains scandalous: not first the sanctuary, but the queue at the shelter, the ward, the visiting room.",
        },
      ],
